<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<RunSettings>
    <DataCollectionRunSettings>
        <DataCollectors>
            <DataCollector friendlyName="XPlat code coverage">
                <Configuration>
                    <Format>cobertura</Format>

                    <!--
                      Excluded from coverage because none of it is shipped, hand-written
                      product code:
                        *.Tests       - the test suites themselves
                        LambdaWebTest - local sample/harness apps, not shipped

                      The *.Testing packages (Hardened.Amz.Shared.Lambda.Testing,
                      Hardened.Amz.Function.Lambda.Testing, Hardened.Amz.Function.DDB.Testing,
                      Hardened.Amz.Function.Sqs.Testing) are deliberately NOT excluded - they
                      ship to consumers and their coverage counts.

                      CSharpAuthor, Hardened.SourceGenerator and ValidationModules all ship as
                      source: their build targets compile src/**/*.cs into whichever assembly
                      references them, which is what keeps a Roslyn analyzer self-contained. The
                      two generator assemblies here are 97% that borrowed source - 6737 lines of
                      it against roughly 170 of their own - so the gate was measuring three other
                      libraries and reporting the number as Hardened.Amz's:

                        Hardened.Amz.Function.Lambda.SourceGenerator   35.6% measured, 97.8% own
                        Hardened.Amz.Web.Lambda.SourceGenerator        46.9% measured, 97.7% own

                      It also moved for reasons that have nothing to do with this repository.
                      Tracking Hardened.Framework 0.4.0 after being stuck on a 1.0.0-preview from
                      before the 0.1.0 line brought in Links/, Templates/ and the compiled route
                      constraints, and CSharpAuthor went 1.1.1006 to 1.1.1010 - so the denominator
                      grew by framework code this repository does not execute, and the gate failed
                      a pull request in which nothing became less tested.

                      Scoped to [Hardened.Amz.*] so this repository's own namespaces are untouched:
                      Hardened.Amz.* stays measured, Hardened.SourceGenerator.* does not. The type
                      filters matter here - both start with "Hardened".

                      Hardened.Framework carries the same filters for the same reason.

                      Hardened.Idl.* is listed because the borrowed source is not all under one
                      namespace. Hardened.SourceGenerator 0.9.0-rc1000 began shipping
                      GZipLiteral.cs, which OpenApiDocumentSource.cs has always used and which
                      0.8.0-rc1000 omitted from the package - that omission was a build failure
                      for every consumer, fixed in Hardened.Framework#151. It declares
                      namespace Hardened.Idl, so the filter above did not reach it and one
                      borrowed file landed in the denominator of two assemblies whose own code is
                      about 170 lines. That is enough to move the gate a long way:

                        Hardened.Amz.Function.Lambda.SourceGenerator  line 97.7 -> 91.3, branch 84.3 -> 71.0
                        Hardened.Amz.Web.Lambda.SourceGenerator       line 97.7 -> 87.2, branch 83.3 -> 55.5

                      Excluded rather than re-baselined, deliberately. Lowering the floor would
                      record a permanent drop in this repository's own coverage to account for a
                      file it does not execute and did not write, and would hide a real regression
                      in the ~170 lines that are actually its own. Match by namespace, not by
                      assembly: this source is compiled into Hardened.Amz.* assemblies, so there is
                      no separate assembly to filter on.

                      Hardened.Generation.* is the same borrowed source under the name it took in
                      Hardened.Framework 0.13.0-rc1000. It had been Hardened.Idl.Shared, which was
                      wrong twice over: nothing referenced it as an assembly, and it held the
                      operation model code-first builds as well - so the rename says what it is.
                      The filter has to follow, and the cost of it not following is the same
                      failure as before, larger, because the shared model is 26 files rather than
                      one:

                        Hardened.Amz.Function.Lambda.SourceGenerator  line 97.7 -> 23.9, branch 84.3 -> 2.4
                        Hardened.Amz.Web.Lambda.SourceGenerator       line 97.7 -> 21.0, branch 83.3 -> 3.7

                      Hardened.Idl.* stays listed. The IDL front ends keep that namespace for what
                      is genuinely IDL, and a later package may ship some of it as source again.
                    -->
                    <Exclude>[*.Tests]*,[LambdaWebTest*]*,[Hardened.Amz.*]CSharpAuthor.*,[Hardened.Amz.*]Hardened.SourceGenerator.*,[Hardened.Amz.*]Hardened.Idl.*,[Hardened.Amz.*]Hardened.Generation.*,[Hardened.Amz.*]ValidationModules.*</Exclude>

                    <!--
                      Source-generated output is verified through behaviour, not by line
                      coverage of emitted files. This matters most in this repository:
                      StreamingEventSerializerContext alone is ~1,400 lines of
                      System.Text.Json generated source that otherwise dominates the numbers.
                    -->
                    <ExcludeByAttribute>Obsolete,GeneratedCodeAttribute,CompilerGeneratedAttribute</ExcludeByAttribute>
                    <ExcludeByFile>**/*.g.cs</ExcludeByFile>

                    <SkipAutoProps>true</SkipAutoProps>
                    <DeterministicReport>true</DeterministicReport>
                </Configuration>
            </DataCollector>
        </DataCollectors>
    </DataCollectionRunSettings>
</RunSettings>
